WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:06.200
CEO’s have a reputation of being douchebags.
They mistreat their employees, exploit every

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:12.011
tax loophole known to mankind, and they aren’t
scared to put profits before ethics. Basically

00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:17.220
every single well known founder has this reputation
including Jeff Bezos, Bill Gates, and Mark

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.949
Zucerkberg. Even the most cherished of the
bunch like Elon Musk display many of these

00:00:21.949 --> 00:00:27.339
characteristics. I mean 80 to 100 hour weeks
are basically the definition of Tesla and

00:00:27.339 --> 00:00:32.399
SpaceX’s work cultures. Many have argued
that though these CEOs have produced tremendous

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:37.539
results, it wasn’t without sacrificing their
humanity. And it often seems like such results

00:00:37.539 --> 00:00:43.670
are simply not possible without being a brutal
businessman. But in reality, hundreds of CEO’s

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:48.940
defy this notion with one of the most notable
ones being Southwest CEO Gary C Kelly. In

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:53.530
2021, Gary ranked number 4 on Glassdoor’s
employee choice list with an approval rating

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:59.800
of 98%. Gary hasn’t dropped the ball on
investors either. Since he took over, Southwest

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.470
stock has quadrupled at the peak. And let’s
keep in mind, this isn’t a tech company,

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:09.070
it’s an airline company. So, here’s how
Gary stayed on the good side of his employees

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:12.810
while growing Southwest in one of the most
cutthroat and low profit industries in the

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:20.700
world. Taking a look back, Gary joined Southwest
way back in 1986 as a modest controller, not

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.049
to be confused with flight controller. By
trade, Gary was an accountant who graduated

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:29.640
from the University of Texas at Austin. Before
joining Southwest, Gary had actually been

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:35.530
working at an auditing company called Arthur
Young &amp; Co. So, becoming a controller at Southwest

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.979
was right up his alley. At the time, Southwest
was a rookie in the airline business who was

00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:44.619
simply trying to get ahead. The company had
just gone public a few years ago, and each

00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:48.890
share of the company was worth less than a
dollar in comparison to today’s prices.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:52.210
Given the small-scale nature of Southwest,
it wasn’t too difficult for Gary to work

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:58.020
himself up to CFO by 1989, and this is where
he would spend the majority of his early career.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.759
It wasn’t until 2001 that Gary was promoted
to Executive Vice President, but his next

00:02:02.759 --> 00:02:08.340
promotion came rather quickly in 2004 to CEO.
Since he became CEO, he’s embraced a couple

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:12.550
of key fundamental values that have cemented
Southwest in the hearts of its employees and

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:17.580
customers starting with a culture of appreciation.
In large companies that employ tens of thousands

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:22.860
of employees, the individual impact of each
employee is often lost. Every employee is

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:28.340
simply a cog in the larger machine and it
often feels like they’re dispensable/replaceable.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:33.400
But Gary has made sure to emphasize that this
is not the case. And I don’t mean just verbally

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:38.880
thanking employees, Gary has backed up his
words with clear action. Throughout Southwest’s

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.950
entire history, they have never laid off a
single employee, not after 9/11, not during

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:48.420
the financial crisis, and not even during
the pandemic. This doesn’t mean that Southwest

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:52.810
just flew through these crises though. In
fact, they were extremely challenging, and

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.730
Southwest had to enact several cost cutting
measures. However, layoffs and pay cuts to

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:01.590
regular employees were always a last resort.
I mean just take a look at how Gary handled

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:06.100
the pandemic. When the pandemic rolled around,
it was clear that Southwest had to make massive

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:11.040
reductions in cost given that air travel demand
literally went to 0 overnight. Instead of

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.040
laying off employees though, Gary targeted
the other side of the spectrum, employees

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.630
that were over 55 and approaching retirement.
Gary offered these pilots over the age of

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:25.640
55 2/3s of their salaries for 5 years or until
they were 65 for simply choosing early retirement.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.030
On top of this extremely generous compensation
plan, Gary also offered up to 5 years of company

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:34.840
provided health insurance. Meanwhile, employees
who were under 55, but had over 10 years of

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.140
experience with Southwest were offered 1 year
of salary and 1 year of company paid health

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:44.510
insurance. Given how lucrative this offer
was, 17,000 employees or 28% of their workforce

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:49.451
took the buyout which was quite close to Gary’s
30% target. I think you’d agree that this

00:03:49.451 --> 00:03:53.930
was literally the most generous way Southwest
could’ve reduced their workforce. On top

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:57.730
of giving employees a great send off, Gary
and all the executives took a paycut through

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:02.700
the end of 2021. Gary’s salary was cut to
$0 while the other executives' salaries were

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:08.540
slashed 20%. Gary also needed regular employees
to take a pay cut as well, but he didn’t

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:13.220
want to impose it. So, he promised that employees
who took a 10% pay cut would have zero risk

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:18.010
of being furloughed in 2021, and that their
pay would be restored to normal starting in

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:22.710
2022. Throughout the entire crisis, there
was no force or imposing. The approach was

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:29.139
always, how do we get through this as a team.
Maybe older employees can take early retirement,

00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:33.790
executives can take a larger pay cut, and
employees can take a smaller pay cut if they

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:37.700
can afford it. Southwest has approached every
crisis with this same team-like approach which

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:42.110
has made the community extremely strong. And
that brings us to Gary’s second strategy

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:46.889
which is breaking down the executive barrier.
As companies grow larger, they often have

00:04:46.889 --> 00:04:51.200
a tendency to become bureaucratic, and the
key reason for this is little to no decision

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:56.710
making power for everyday employees. At Southwest,
however, the decision making process is completely

00:04:56.710 --> 00:05:00.800
decentralized. As we saw with the pandemic,
it was up to the employees if they wanted

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.030
to take a 10% pay cut and avoid a furlough
or if they wanted to keep their full salary

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:09.520
and deal with the risk of a potential furlough.
This decentralization stretches much further

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.000
than just allowing employees to choose their
own future though. Employees often make significant

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.669
decisions for the entire company itself. A
great example was when Southwest was developing

00:05:14.669 --> 00:05:19.349
a new uniform for their employees. Pilot and
flight attendant uniforms are extremely important

00:05:19.349 --> 00:05:23.789
as this is what customers see on a daily basis,
and it’s essentially the branding of the

00:05:23.789 --> 00:05:27.830
entire company. Given the weight of this redesign,
you would think that Southwest would hire

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:32.530
top tier branding specialists to guide the
process, but Gary had something else in mind.

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:36.979
Gary opened up the redesign opportunity to
all Southwest employees. Every employee who

00:05:36.979 --> 00:05:40.689
was interested could provide suggestions for
the new uniform and the employees with best

00:05:40.689 --> 00:05:44.720
best suggestions were assembled into a sort
of review team. Every two weeks, this team

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:49.289
would meet up and guide the redesign process.
Similarly, a significant amount of decision

00:05:49.289 --> 00:05:54.199
making power is given to low level managers
and employees. Gary believes that these individuals

00:05:54.199 --> 00:05:58.099
are the ones that have the best understanding
of the work they do, and that they can make

00:05:58.099 --> 00:06:02.840
way better decisions than some executive that
spends their entire day in the office. All

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:06.879
of this is based on Gary’s core idea that
employee satisfaction trumps everything even

00:06:06.879 --> 00:06:11.740
including customer satisfaction. The truth
is customer satisfaction is directly linked

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:15.719
to employee satisfaction, so if you keep your
employees happy, customer satisfaction will

00:06:15.719 --> 00:06:20.349
follow. This doesn’t mean that Gary doesn’t
care about customers though. If anything,

00:06:20.349 --> 00:06:24.270
he cares about each individual customer just
as much as he cares about each individual

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:27.990
employee. If you’ve ever taken a Southwest
flight, you might’ve noticed that they don’t

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:33.560
have business or first class, they only have
economy. Originally, this was because Southwest

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:38.319
was a budget airline and premium flyers weren’t
interested in the company. But today, Southwest

00:06:38.319 --> 00:06:42.220
actively chooses against adding first and
business classes. Now, Southwest does offer

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:46.070
something called business select, but this
doesn’t actually get you a better seat or

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:49.990
service. Business select simply allows you
to move up the boarding list and it gives

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:54.189
you some additional member rewards. However,
you’ll be sitting in the same seat and receiving

00:06:54.189 --> 00:06:58.629
the same service as someone with an economy
ticket. By opting for no first and business

00:06:58.629 --> 00:07:02.279
classes, Gary has been able to prevent an
executive barrier from forming amongst customers

00:07:02.279 --> 00:07:06.939
and service. It’s no secret that most airlines
make the majority of their profit from business

00:07:06.939 --> 00:07:11.129
and first class flyers thanks to how much
margin is built into those services. However,

00:07:11.129 --> 00:07:15.320
this of course creates a large discrepancy
in how customers are treated which goes against

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:19.539
Southwest’s core principle of valuing each
customer equally. Customers who fly Southwest

00:07:19.539 --> 00:07:24.059
generally love this aspect. Flight attendants
aren’t prioritizing business and first class

00:07:24.059 --> 00:07:28.439
members, and economy customers are the center
of attention. Now, I’m not saying that it’s

00:07:28.439 --> 00:07:32.219
wrong to prioritize business and first class
customers given that they’re paying way

00:07:32.219 --> 00:07:36.969
more. However, by simply sticking to economy
customers, Southwest is able to provide an

00:07:36.969 --> 00:07:42.199
extremely consistent and reliable experience
to all customers. And speaking of happy customers,

00:07:42.199 --> 00:07:46.849
we have Gary’s third strategy which is not
nickeling and diming customers or employees.

00:07:46.849 --> 00:07:51.680
Ever since 9/11 and especially after the 2008
financial crisis, more and more american airlines

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:55.730
have been charging additional fees on top
of the ticket price for checked bags. And

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:59.789
as more airlines adopt this strategy, it becomes
harder to compete against them because they’re

00:07:59.789 --> 00:08:04.150
able to advertise cheaper starting prices.
But the truth is, these cheaper prices are

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:08.569
deceptively low considering that most people
are going to need to check in some luggage.

00:08:08.569 --> 00:08:11.629
Southwest has avoided this by simply allowing
all customers to check in two bags for no

00:08:11.629 --> 00:08:15.279
additional cost. This has actually become
one of Southwest’s most famous marketing

00:08:15.279 --> 00:08:21.349
slogans and is an integral part of the brand
today. Unfortunately, Southwest can’t actually

00:08:21.349 --> 00:08:25.589
offer this service for free as it costs money
to transport luggage, and this leads to Southwest's

00:08:25.589 --> 00:08:29.740
starting prices to generally be a bit higher
than competitors. But Southwest customers

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:33.300
can be confident that the ticket price is
all they’ll have to pay. There aren’t

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:37.190
any hidden or additional fees that they find
out about when they arrive at the airport.

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:41.400
This policy also extends to other parts of
Southwest’s business. For example, they

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:45.510
don’t charge any change fees or cancellation
fees. If you want to switch from one ticket

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:50.010
to another, you’ll only have to pay the
disparity in ticket price if there is one.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:53.910
By not nickeling and diming customers, Gary
has been able to largely eliminate customer

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:58.980
complaints. In fact, Southwest only receives
about ½ a complaint for every 100,000 customers

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:03.511
they serve which is by far the lowest in the
airline industry. Gary also doesn’t nickel

00:09:03.511 --> 00:09:08.170
and dime his employees either. Right before
the pandemic started, Southwest dispersed

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:13.620
$667 million worth of profits to employees
which came out to roughly 12.2% of each employee’s

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:17.950
salary. 10% went towards their retirement
plans and the remaining 2.2% was dispersed

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:22.390
as cash. At the end of the day, the secrets
behind Gary’s crazy employee and customer

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:27.220
satisfaction rates aren’t actually all that
crazy. All he does is give his employees and

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:31.300
customers the freedom to make their own decisions,
treats everyone as equals, and he doesn’t

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:35.840
nickel and dime anyone. You would think that
these principles are pretty easy to apply,

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:40.921
but given how many douchebag CEOs there are,
this clearly isn’t the case. But hey, at

00:09:40.921 --> 00:09:45.160
least we have a couple of star performers
like Gary. What do you guys think is the biggest

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:49.890
key to Gary’s popularity amongst employees?
Comment that down below. Also, drop a like

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:54.370
if you think all CEOs should learn a thing
or two from Gary. And of course consider joining

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:57.940
